  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The neutron has a relative mass of 1 and no charge.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Electrons carry a single negative charge, balanced by the proton's positive charge in a neutral atom.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An electron is roughly 1/1840 the mass of a proton, which is why the nucleus holds almost all the mass.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Atomic number Z counts protons and fixes the identity of the element.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mass number A counts the nucleons, protons plus neutrons.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Neutrons = A − Z = 39 − 19 = 20.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A neutral atom has electrons equal to protons, so 17.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Losing two electrons from 12 leaves 10, the configuration of neon.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Isotopes share Z and differ in A, so their neutron counts differ.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chemistry is decided by the electrons, and isotopes have identical electron configurations.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    (75 × 35 + 25 × 37) ÷ 100 = 35.5.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    (79 × 24 + 10 × 25 + 11 × 26) ÷ 100 = 24.32.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is a weighted mean relative to the carbon-12 standard, which is why it need not be a whole number.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Eleven electrons fill the first two shells (2 + 8) and leave one in the third.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    17 electrons give 2 + 8 + 7.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The 4s level lies below 3d, so the nineteenth electron enters a new shell.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    After the argon core the next two electrons enter 4s.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    4s fills before 3d because it is slightly lower in energy.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two electrons may share an orbital only with opposite spins.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Degenerate orbitals take one electron each, with parallel spins, before pairing begins.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One orbital holds at most two electrons, of opposite spin.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The 3p⁵ subshell has one paired and two half-filled orbitals, giving one unpaired electron.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Outer-shell electrons determine the group; the highest shell used gives the period.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Seven outer electrons means Group 7; three shells means Period 3.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two outer electrons give Group 2, and four shells give Period 4 — it is calcium.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The definition specifies gaseous atoms, giving singly positive gaseous ions.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Large jumps appear when a new shell is broken into, proving that electrons are arranged in groups.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sharp lines mean fixed energy differences, so electrons can occupy only particular levels.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its steady radioactive decay allows the age of organic remains to be estimated.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its gamma radiation destroys tumour cells and sterilises instruments.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    24 − 12 = 12 neutrons.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Gaining one electron gives 18.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Losing three electrons leaves 10.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Same element, different mass number.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sixteen electrons, ending 3s² 3p⁴, is sulphur.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The configuration ends at 4p, so four shells are used.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Three outer electrons and three shells.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
